sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2014-tumor-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The endogenous chloride intracellular channel 1 (CLIC1) is required for chloride-driven, long-range suppression of oncogene-induced tumor-like structures in Xenopus laevis, because non-local overexpression of wildtype CLIC1 increases suppression while a dominant-negative CLIC1C24S mutant blocks suppression even under high extracellular chloride.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Native CLIC1 mediates chloride-based long-range tumor suppression";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Chernet BT, Levin M, “Transmembrane voltage potential of somatic cells controls oncogene-mediated tumorigenesis at long-range”, Oncotarget (2014), doi:10.18632/oncotarget.1935; section “RESULTS › Chloride-dependent hyperpolarization through endogenous chloride intracellular channel 1 (CLIC1)”. Scope: 16-cell-stage Xenopus laevis embryos injected with KRASG12D plus wildtype or dominant-negative CLIC1 in a separate blastomere, raised in 70 mM chloride"
}
